WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.525
Bounds safety starts with choosing the right representation.

2
00:00:02.525 --> 00:00:09.786
Standard containers such as vector, array, and string are usually easier to reason about than raw arrays because they carry size information and

3
00:00:09.786 --> 00:00:10.418
manage storage.

4
00:00:10.418 --> 00:00:16.100
They are not magic shields, but they reduce the amount of handwritten bookkeeping that developers must get right.

5
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.835
When code must work near low-level buffers, it should track size and capacity explicitly.

6
00:00:20.835 --> 00:00:22.729
Capacity is how much storage exists.

7
00:00:22.729 --> 00:00:26.202
Size or used length is how much meaningful data is present.

8
00:00:26.202 --> 00:00:31.253
Confusing those values can lead to reading uninitialized data, overwriting memory, or truncating information without noticing.

9
00:00:31.253 --> 00:00:34.725
Range checks should happen before indexing, copying, parsing, or converting data.

10
00:00:34.725 --> 00:00:37.251
A check after the access is too late.

11
00:00:37.251 --> 00:00:45.143
Reviewers should look for how an index was derived, whether it can be negative or wrap through conversion, whether the container can change size, and

12
00:00:45.143 --> 00:00:48.299
whether the check still applies at the point of use.

13
00:00:48.299 --> 00:00:54.929
Non-owning view types such as span and string_view are useful because they pair a pointer-like view with a length.

14
00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:58.401
But they do not extend the lifetime of the referenced data.

15
00:00:58.401 --> 00:01:06.609
A string_view returned from a temporary string, or a span stored beyond the lifetime of the array it views, can become a clean-looking lifetime bug.

16
00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:08.503
C-style strings require special discipline.

17
00:01:08.503 --> 00:01:13.870
They depend on null termination, but many data sources provide byte ranges rather than valid C strings.

18
00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:21.762
Before using string-only operations, the code should know that the data is terminated, that the terminator is within bounds, and that binary data is

19
00:01:21.762 --> 00:01:23.340
not being treated as text.

20
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.444
Secure review should distinguish owning containers, borrowed views, raw buffers, and string data.

21
00:01:27.444 --> 00:01:29.654
Each has different lifetime and bounds rules.

22
00:01:29.654 --> 00:01:38.809
The goal is not to forbid low-level code; it is to make low-level assumptions explicit and keep them out of places where a safer abstraction would do

23
00:01:38.809 --> 00:01:39.440
the job.
